From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 04:02:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] soc: apple: sep: xart: fence, read back and disarm gigalocker
 writes; add strict guard

Harden every path by which the driver writes the shared xART gigalocker in
the iBoot System Container, the only state it writes that macOS reads:

- Opening never writes. repair_disk() ran at open with writes armed and
  deleted slots without the guard. It could only ever find nothing (arming
  already requires no malformed or duplicate record, and after the scan
  every slot with a nonzero kind is one of those or live), so it is removed
  rather than guarded.
- A write fence: every byte the driver writes goes through put(), which
  allows one whole 36 KiB slot or one slot's first 4 KiB block, starting on
  a slot boundary inside the APFS-resolved 6 MiB extent (fence(), checked
  arithmetic, ERANGE otherwise). No write can reach the container's APFS
  metadata or another file.
- Read-back before the old record goes: after the new slot is written and
  flushed, the page cache is dropped and the slot is read back from the
  device. On a mismatch the old record stays authoritative, the new slot's
  header is taken back, and the write fails with EIO.
- Any write, flush or read-back failure disarms writes for the rest of the
  boot (Touch ID unavailable until a restart; no further record changes).
- One rewrite of an existing record per boot in all: the first-setup
  commit, the policy and create windows and the one-time allowance now
  share the cap (before, a fresh install's first create could let two
  existing kind-4 records through), and the first-setup commit saves the
  record's previous value first, as the windows do.
- A strict guard (xart_strict, default: the platform's choice): no
  rewrite or delete of a session record that existed before Linux created
  it, by any window, first setup or allowance; the allowance file is left
  unused. The shared roots still follow xart_guard's level.
- xart_writes and provision_keybag become tri-state (-1 = the platform's
  default; provision follows xart_writes), so a platform can stay read-only
  until the owner turns Touch ID on. Every profile keeps today's default.

The store is generic over its block file and the guard's list file, so the
write path runs in KUnit against an in-memory container with injected
faults (apple_sep_xart_store: fence bounds; no write at open; read-only
store; writes stay inside the store; failed write, torn write, failed
flush and silent corruption each keep every other record and every byte
outside the store unchanged and fail closed; strict never changes another
OS's record; an unsaved previous value refuses the rewrite; the
first-setup commit is the boot's only rewrite), and apple_sep_xart_guard
gains the strict and cap cases. One existing case changes meaning with
the cap: an allowance now uses the boot's one rewrite, so the create
window has none left.

Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
